    "q-331491": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331491",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A bag contains $5$ green marbles, $7$ yellow marbles, and $8$ black marbles. An experiment of randomly selecting one marble from the bag and putting it back is repeated $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a black marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a black marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ selections.\n\n- The probability of selecting a black marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{5+7+8}=\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.276,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
