    "q-89399": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89399",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "It is known that $41%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people with blood type O+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ trials\n\n- the probability of finding a person with blood type O+ is\n\n$$\np=0.41\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.41).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(1-0.41)}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(0.59)}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a person with blood type O+) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2}){(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3}){(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & ={(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+10{(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+45{(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+120{(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & =0.358\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
